  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe proposed approach uses a multi-level optimization strategy combining gradient-based and derivative-free methods to explore the solution space more thoroughly. Starting from the initial_h_values, we first refine the solution using a local optimization method, then switch to a derivative-free method (Nelder-Mead) to escape from local minima. By increasing the resolution of the discretization and using multiple randomized restarts, we enhance the exploration of potential solutions.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Variables: h \u2208 [0, 1]^n with \u2211h = n/2\n- Objective: min max_k (h \u2217 (1 - h))(k) * dx\n- Constraints:\n  - 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n  - \u2211h = n/2\n- Upsampling: Increase n to 500 while maintaining initial structure\n- Solver: scipy.optimize.minimize with Nelder-Mead\n- Feasibility projection: h = clip(h, 0, 1) then normalize sum to n/2\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n1. Initialize with initial_h_values at n=47\n2. Upsample to n=500 by interpolating values\n3. For 5 iterations:\n   a. Apply Nelder-Mead optimization\n   b. Add random perturbations to the best solution\n   c. Reproject to feasible region\n   d. Validate and track the best C5\n4. Perform final feasibility check and evaluation\n5. Return the best solution and C5\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Nelder-Mead parameters: initial simplex size 0.1, max iterations 200\n- Perturbation strength: 5% of h values\n- Upscaling by interpolation preserves initial structure\n- Validate after every 10 iterations\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Total time: 600s\n- Nelder-Mead iterations: 200 * 500 evaluations = 100,000 operations\n- Robustness: Feasibility checks and perturbation ensure exploration\n- Fallback to best solution if time limit reached\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Ensure 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and \u2211h = n/2\n- Recompute C5 using evaluate_erdos_solution\n- Check for NaNs or Infs\n- Provide the best h_values, C5, and n_points",
